<h4 id="Explanation:"><strong>Explanation:</strong><a class="anchor-link" href="#Explanation:">¶</a></h4><p><strong>What is WCSS?</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>WCSS = Within-Cluster Sum of Squares</li>
<li>It measures how tightly the data points are clustered around the cluster center.</li>
<li>Smaller WCSS means better clustering (but too small means we might be overfitting).</li>
<li>As k increases, WCSS always decreases.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Plotting the Elbow Curve:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>This plots k vs. WCSS.</li>
<li>Look for the elbow point, where WCSS starts to decrease slowly.</li>
<li>The elbow point is considered the optimal number of clusters (k).</li>
<li>After the elbow, adding more clusters doesn't improve the model much.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Why is it important?</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Helps us avoid underfitting (too few clusters) and overfitting (too many clusters).</li>
<li>Gives us a data-driven way to choose k instead of guessing.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
