<h2 id="Step-10:-Interpretation-&amp;-Insights">Step 10: Interpretation &amp; Insights<a class="anchor-link" href="#Step-10:-Interpretation-&amp;-Insights">¶</a></h2><ul>
<li>K-Means showed well-separated and balanced clusters.</li>
<li>DBSCAN detected outliers but may need further parameter tuning.</li>
<li>Agglomerative Clustering provided interpretable hierarchical groupings.</li>
<li>Each clustering approach offers unique insights for marketing decisions.</li>
<li>The PCA plots confirm meaningful separation of customer groups.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="1.-K-Means-Clustering">1. K-Means Clustering<a class="anchor-link" href="#1.-K-Means-Clustering">¶</a></h3><ul>
<li>Formed 4 customer segments.</li>
<li>Cluster 2 appears to be the most valuable segment with customers having the highest income (~81,926), low number of children, and highest spending on wines, meats, and other products.</li>
<li>Cluster 0 represents lower-income customers (~34,263) with moderate recency and very low spending, possibly inactive or less engaged customers.</li>
<li>Cluster 3 has medium-income customers (~57,354) but with relatively high Teenhome values, indicating families with teenagers.</li>
<li>Cluster 1 has good income (~74,182) and moderate spending, could represent middle-tier customers who are active but not top spenders.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="2.-DBSCAN-Clustering">2. DBSCAN Clustering<a class="anchor-link" href="#2.-DBSCAN-Clustering">¶</a></h3><ul>
<li>Detected many small clusters and a large noise cluster (-1).</li>
<li>Cluster -1 is likely the majority and includes customers with diverse spending and income patterns but average recency and moderate purchasing.</li>
<li>Some small clusters (like Cluster 5, 6) identify niche customer groups with specific characteristics, such as Cluster 5 having no kids, high spending, and low recency, which could indicate very loyal high-value customers.</li>
<li>DBSCAN detected outliers and customers that don't fit well into dense clusters, which K-Means might have forced into a cluster.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="3.-Agglomerative-Clustering">3. Agglomerative Clustering<a class="anchor-link" href="#3.-Agglomerative-Clustering">¶</a></h3><ul>
<li>Formed 4 distinct segments.</li>
<li>Cluster 1 has customers with the highest income (~78,587) and the highest spending across all product categories, making it a premium segment.</li>
<li>Cluster 2 represents lower-income customers (~32,893) with high Kidhome, suggesting families with children but lower spending power.</li>
<li>Cluster 0 shows moderate income and moderate spending, likely representing the average or stable customer segment.</li>
<li>Cluster 3 has a relatively good income but slightly lower spending than Cluster 1, could be value-conscious or less engaged customers.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="Overall-Insights">Overall Insights<a class="anchor-link" href="#Overall-Insights">¶</a></h3><ul>
<li>All three methods show that there are clear segments based on income, family size (Kidhome &amp; Teenhome), and spending behavior.</li>
<li>High-income customers tend to have lower Kidhome and higher spending on wines and meat products.</li>
<li>Low-income or larger families tend to have lower spending across categories.</li>
<li>DBSCAN revealed outliers and small but important niche groups that are not visible in K-Means or Agglomerative Clustering.</li>
<li>Agglomerative clustering gave very clean and interpretable clusters that seem aligned with real-world customer profiles.</li>
</ul>
